{
 "_note": "The Hebrew letters as an engine letter table: 22 letters and their five final forms. EVIDENCE carried from ../GoldenDawnBlocks/data/letters.json (the Sefer Yetsirah's 3/7/12 division, the mothers' elements, the doubles' two aspects, the simples' faculties, gematria). ADDED here, and ours: every ordinary letter joins forward (a written word is one body), and the five final forms SEVER (a body ends where a final form stands -- Hebrew's visible word-end; GoldenDawnBlocks calls it SEAL). No other primitive is given to a Hebrew letter: what these letters do as blocks is not yet derived.",
 "sources": [
  {
   "kind": "CORPUS",
   "cite": "Marla Segol, Word and Image in Medieval Kabbalah (Palgrave Macmillan, 2012), translating a Sefer Yetsirah diagram manuscript, ff. 17b–18a",
   "supports": "The list of the twelve simples and their faculties; that each of the seven doubles has two aspects; and that a simple letter can do 'neither cure nor harm on its own' but acts when letters gather together."
  },
  {
   "kind": "SOURCE",
   "cite": "Sefer Yetsirah, the 3/7/12 division and the mothers' elements",
   "supports": "That the alphabet divides into three mothers, seven doubles and twelve simples, and that Aleph, Mem and Shin are given air, water and fire."
  }
 ],
 "divisions": {
  "three_seven_twelve": "The Sefer Yetsirah's own division of the alphabet: three mothers, seven doubles, twelve simples. 3+7+12=22 exactly, which is arithmetic rather than assertion.",
  "finals": "The five letters that take a different form at the end of a word. Plain orthography.",
  "gematria": "1..400 across the 22; the finals extend the series to 900 in some usages."
 },
 "interpretation": {
  "connects_forward": "INTERPRETATION: Hebrew script does not join on the page; the bodies here follow the SY's \"combined one with another\", not the ductus.",
  "finals_sever": "INTERPRETATION: the final form is the word-end made visible, so it ends a body. Derived from the page, like the Arabic non-connecting letters; the mapping to SEVER is ours."
 },
